<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # My Advice for a Ninth Grader Is... – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 10th Grade (CEFR: B1.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Academic Achievement and Skill Development</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> My Advice for a Ninth Grader Is...</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Analyze informational content for specific data. Reading: Make basic inferences or predictions about a text content from headings, titles, or headlines.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can analyze informational texts about academic success to find specific study tips and strategies (e.g., "The article says that we should create a study schedule to manage our time better."). Can make predictions about a text's content based on its title or headings (e.g., "From the title 'Top 5 Study Tips for Success,' I think the article will suggest ideas like setting goals or taking breaks."). Can infer the purpose of a section in a study guide by analyzing headings and key phrases (e.g., "The heading 'Time Management Strategies' suggests this section will explain how to organize study time effectively.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays various images of students engaged in different academic activities (e.g., studying in a library, participating in class, organizing their notes). Students describe what they see. The teacher then asks: "What actions do you think lead to academic achievement?" and "What advice would you give to a younger student who wants to improve their study skills?" Students share ideas in pairs, activating prior knowledge related to the theme "My Advice for a Ninth Grader Is...".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to give advice using target grammar. For example: "If I were a ninth grader, I would organize my time better to achieve my goals." "You should focus on developing good study habits from the beginning." "You must track your progress consistently." The teacher pronounces key vocabulary (e.g., "achievement," "growth," "skill," "progress" (noun /PRO-gress/ vs. verb /pro-GRESS/)) focusing on contrastive sounds like /gr/ in "growth" and /kr/ in "create", and stress patterns. Students listen and repeat.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say, 'You should create a study schedule,' am I telling you something mandatory or a suggestion?" (Expected answer: A suggestion)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say, 'If I studied harder, I would get better grades,' am I talking about a real situation or a hypothetical one?" (Expected answer: A hypothetical one)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: Academic Achievement and Skill Development.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> An informational article titled "Essential Strategies for Ninth Grade Success" or "Top Tips for Academic Growth". The text is divided into sections with clear headings (e.g., "Time Management," "Effective Study Habits," "Seeking Feedback"). It contains instances of second conditional for advice, modals for advice, and first conditional, along with target vocabulary (e.g., <i>achievement, skill, development, advice, study, goals, improvement, practice, focus, feedback, growth, strategy, effort, progress, plan, discipline, success, time management, motivation</i>).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will demonstrate the ability to analyze informational texts for specific data and infer the purpose of text sections based on headings and key phrases. This aligns with reading comprehension for academic purposes.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Prediction from Title/Headings:</b> Before reading, students read the article's title ("Essential Strategies for Ninth Grade Success") and section headings. In pairs, they predict what information each section will cover. For example: "From the title, I think the article will suggest ideas like setting goals or taking breaks." (Learning Outcome 2).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Reading:</b> Students quickly skim the article (2 minutes) to get the general idea. They then answer a gist question: "What is the main purpose of this article?" (e.g., "to provide general advice," "to list specific study techniques").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Specific Data Analysis:</b> Students read the article more carefully to find specific study tips and strategies. They answer questions such as: "According to the article, what is one strategy for better time management?" or "What does the text suggest about seeking feedback?" (Learning Outcome 1).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading for specific information, identifying grammatical structures (second conditional, modals for advice, first conditional), and understanding target vocabulary (<i>achievement, skill, development, advice, study, goals, improvement, practice, focus, feedback, growth, strategy, effort, progress, plan, discipline, success, time management, motivation</i>) within a text about academic success.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Vocabulary Matching & Sentence Completion:</b> The teacher provides a list of key vocabulary words from the article (e.g., <i>strategy, discipline, feedback, procrastinate, achieve</i>). Students match these words to their definitions. Then, they complete sentences related to academic success using the correct vocabulary word from the list, based on their understanding from the text. For example: "A good ____ for studying is to break tasks into smaller parts." (Expected: strategy).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Grammar Identification in Context:</b> Students re-read specific paragraphs of the article and highlight or underline all instances of:
                        <ul style="list-style-type: lower-alpha; padding-left: 20px;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Modals for advice (e.g., "You <b>should</b> create a study schedule...")</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Second conditional for hypothetical advice (e.g., "If I <b>were</b> more organized, I <b>would achieve</b> my academic goals...")</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">First conditional for consequences (e.g., "If you <b>focus</b> on your studies, you <b>will improve</b>...")</li>
                        </ul>
                        They then discuss in pairs what type of advice each structure conveys.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Inferring Purpose & True/False:</b> Students work in pairs to re-read each section heading and the corresponding paragraph. They infer the main purpose of each section (e.g., "The heading 'Time Management Strategies' suggests this section will explain how to organize study time effectively."). Following this, they answer True/False questions based on specific details from the article to check their reading comprehension, correcting false statements with information from the text.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: Academic Achievement and Skill Development.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in small groups (3-4 students). Each group is tasked with creating a "Ninth Grader's Guide to Success" digital poster or infographic based on the article they read. The task is to synthesize the most important advice and present it clearly and concisely.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative digital poster/infographic creation (e.g., using Google Slides, Canva, or large paper). Each group must:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Re-read the article "Essential Strategies for Ninth Grade Success" to extract the top 5-7 most important pieces of advice.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Summarize each piece of advice using target grammar (modals like "You should...", "You must...", "You can...", and second conditional for hypothetical advice like "If you were more proactive, you would see greater growth.") and vocabulary (<i>strategy, plan, discipline, focus, improvement, motivation, time management, feedback</i>).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Organize their poster logically with clear headings (e.g., "Manage Your Time," "Set Achievable Goals").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Prepare to briefly present their guide to the class, explaining their selected advice and why it's important for academic achievement.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher circulates, facilitating peer group discussions on academic success strategies and providing clear, actionable feedback while observing students' use of inclusive language.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during group work and a quick peer assessment during presentations. The teacher will focus on how well students have analyzed the informational text and synthesized the advice.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students present their "Ninth Grader's Guide to Success" poster to the class. Peers listen and evaluate the clarity and relevance of the advice presented.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accuracy of extracted study tips/strategies from the original article.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and organization of the advice on the poster.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (e.g., <i>time management, strategy, focus, discipline</i>) in summaries.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of modals for advice and second conditional structures.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaboration and engagement within the group.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following prompts individually:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was the most challenging part of analyzing the article for specific study tips, and how did you overcome it?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">If you had to give one piece of advice to a peer based on today's reading, what would it be and why?</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How did predicting the content of the article from its headings help you understand it better?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Which specific reading strategies (e.g., skimming for gist, scanning for details, inferring purpose) did you find most useful today, and why?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in helping students analyze the informational text and identify specific advice? (e.g., the structure of the article, the explicit modeling of grammar).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for the next reading lesson, especially for students who struggled with making inferences or analyzing specific data? Perhaps more guided practice with text structures.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did students successfully integrate the target vocabulary and grammatical structures from the text into their advice?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson focused on reading and comprehending academic advice. In the next lesson (Lesson 3: Speaking & Interaction), students will actively use the extracted advice and the target linguistic structures to discuss study strategies, share their own academic experiences, and provide advice to peers in interactive speaking tasks, building on the vocabulary and ideas from this reading lesson.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>